---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import heroPhoto from "../../assets/photos/DSC08846-Edit_cropped.jpg";
import OwnershipActions from "./OwnershipActions.astro";

interface Props {
  basePath: string;
}

const { basePath } = Astro.props;
const contactSubject = "Power BI & Fabric - where it's stuck";
---

<section class="o-section o-hero" aria-labelledby="power-bi-heading">
  <div class="o-wrap o-hero-grid">
    <div class="o-hero-copy">
      <p class="o-eyebrow">Working together on Power BI and Fabric</p>
      <h1 id="power-bi-heading">Reporting your team <em>can own.</em></h1>
      <p class="o-lead">I work alongside your team so they can understand the reporting they run, build what they need and check the effect of a change.</p>
      <p class="o-intro">I'm Alan Harman-Box. We can work with a whole team, a few analysts or one person.</p>
      <OwnershipActions placement="hero" subject={contactSubject} />
    </div>
    <figure class="o-hero-photo">
      <Picture src={heroPhoto} alt="Alan in a red checked overshirt, smiling, in a narrow brick alley" formats={["avif"]} fallbackFormat="webp" widths={[400, 600, 800]} sizes="(min-width: 62rem) 42vw, 100vw" quality={70} loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
      <figcaption class="o-caption">Alan Harman-Box · burningsuit</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="o-section o-situations" aria-labelledby="power-bi-situations-heading">
  <div class="o-wrap">
    <div class="o-section-head">
      <p class="o-eyebrow">Where we start</p>
      <h2 id="power-bi-situations-heading">What do you need to be able to do?</h2>
      <p>You might recognise one of these, or a mixture. If the reporting requirements are still unclear, <a href="#discovery">Discovery gives us a place to begin</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="o-situation-list">
      <article class="o-situation" id="skills">
        <span class="o-number" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <h3>We need to develop our skills</h3>
        <p>We agree what people need to be able to do, then teach the Power BI skills that get them there. If you're arranging training, the aim might be a team writing and checking its own DAX, maintaining a model or distributing reports to the right people.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="o-situation" id="delivery">
        <span class="o-number" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <h3>We've got reporting to deliver</h3>
        <p>There's a report or model you need, and the team wants to build it. I teach the next thing the project needs; your people apply it between sessions. We work through the difficult bits together, with the team's hands on the keyboard.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="o-situation" id="inherited-systems">
        <span class="o-number" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <h3>Someone else built our system</h3>
        <p>The platform is running, but handover documents only go so far. I help the team follow a number through the model, understand why it was built that way, and practise making and checking changes. This can run alongside an external build or after handover.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="o-situation" id="leader-support">
        <span class="o-number" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
        <h3>I'd like a second opinion</h3>
        <p>If you're responsible for reporting and Power BI isn't your specialism, a regular slot of your own can help. We can look at the architecture, a hire or a sign-off; or work through the scope, quotes and handover for an external build.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="o-situation" id="backup">
        <span class="o-number" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
        <h3>We're underway and need backup</h3>
        <p>Your team is building already, and the questions are getting more specific. Office hours give them somewhere to bring an awkward measure, an unexpected result or a change they'd like checked, with email support between sessions.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="o-section o-method" aria-labelledby="power-bi-method-heading">
  <div class="o-wrap">
    <div class="o-section-head">
      <p class="o-eyebrow">Alongside your team</p>
      <h2 id="power-bi-method-heading">How the learning becomes part of the job</h2>
      <p>We can practise on example data shaped like your reporting, work directly on your live systems, or move between the two. The team's readiness, the sensitivity of the data and the work ahead determine where we start.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="o-method-steps">
      <li><h3>Workshops teach the next step</h3><p>Each workshop focuses on a topic the team needs now. I prepare it around your reporting context.</p></li>
      <li><h3>The team tries it between sessions</h3><p>Your people need time to practise and apply what they've learned. They bring back what worked, what didn't and what they'd like to try next.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Office hours work through the problems</h3><p>The team brings whatever is stuck, and we work through it together. Email support runs throughout each month, so questions can come up between calls.</p></li>
      <li><h3>You can have a slot of your own</h3><p>Optional leader sessions make room for the decisions that land on you. They can be part of the team's arrangement or quoted separately.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="o-note">We agree the number and mix of sessions in your quote, then review what helps as the team gets further. People can specialise; no one person needs to understand every part of the stack in the same detail.</p>
    <div class="o-related-links">
      <a class="o-text-link" href="/work/contact-centre/">See a lead build her own reporting <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      <a class="o-text-link" href="/work/law-firm/">See a team take over an external build <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="o-section o-discovery" id="discovery" aria-labelledby="discovery-heading">
  <div class="o-wrap o-discovery-grid">
    <div class="o-section-head">
      <p class="o-eyebrow">When the requirements need working out</p>
      <h2 id="discovery-heading">Discovery</h2>
      <p class="o-price">£1,950 <span>excluding VAT</span></p>
      <p>We start with two conversations: one with the person leading the work, and one with the team. I work out what you need your reporting to show, for whom, and what you want to do differently once you can see the numbers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="o-discovery-outputs">
      <h3>What you take away</h3>
      <ul class="o-output-list">
        <li><strong>Initial reporting requirements:</strong> a first cut of the questions the reporting needs to answer.</li>
        <li><strong>A project roadmap:</strong> the work needed to get from where you are to where you want to be.</li>
        <li><strong>A skills roadmap:</strong> what the team will need to learn to deliver and maintain the reporting.</li>
        <li><strong>An assessment of the ambition:</strong> whether it fits the team and the time you have available.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The requirements and roadmaps are yours to keep. If that gives you enough to carry the work forward internally, we can stop there.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="o-section o-pricing" id="pricing" aria-labelledby="power-bi-pricing-heading">
  <div class="o-wrap">
    <div class="o-section-head">
      <p class="o-eyebrow">Ongoing work</p>
      <h2 id="power-bi-pricing-heading">An arrangement that can change with the team</h2>
      <p>I'll quote for the sessions, preparation, materials and support that your team needs. The price covers the work around the calls as well as our time together.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="o-price-grid o-price-grid-two">
      <article class="o-price-item">
        <h3>Substantial team engagements</h3>
        <p class="o-price">£3,000–£6,000 <span>a month</span></p>
        <p>This is a guide for ongoing work with a team, combining workshops, office hours, preparation, materials and email support in the mix we agree.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="o-price-item">
        <h3>Lighter support</h3>
        <p class="o-price o-price-words">Quoted separately</p>
        <p>If you need fewer sessions, office hours or a regular second opinion, we can arrange that. Lighter support is available below the monthly guide.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="o-note">All prices exclude VAT. The £3,000–£6,000 guide is for substantial team engagements, rather than a minimum spend.</p>
    <div class="o-terms-grid">
      <article><h3>Rolling monthly</h3><p>We book a month at a time, and you stop when it stops being useful.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Booked up front</h3><p>You can book three months or more as a single fixed quote. Discovery is credited only against an upfront booking of at least three months.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Less time as you need less help</h3><p>Either arrangement can taper in time and cost as the team becomes more capable. We agree that change together.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="o-contact-note">
      <p>Tell me what you're trying to make possible. We can work out a useful starting point on a 30-minute call; there's nothing to prepare.</p>
      <OwnershipActions placement="power-bi-engagement" subject={contactSubject} />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="o-section o-ai" id="practical-ai" aria-labelledby="practical-ai-heading">
  <div class="o-wrap o-ai-bridge">
    <div class="o-section-head">
      <p class="o-eyebrow">Practical AI support</p>
      <h2 id="practical-ai-heading">Already have Copilot?</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>We can use the same working relationship to help your team use Copilot in their Power BI work. Workshops focus on tasks worth trying and how to check the output; office hours and email cover what happens as the team puts it to use.</p>
      <p>If the earlier question is where AI would help at all, AI Fit for Teams is a separate two-to-three-week decision project.</p>
      <a class="o-text-link" href="/ai-fit-for-teams/">Read about AI Fit for Teams <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a class="o-text-link o-return-link" href={`${basePath}/`}>Back to the overview <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
